WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.460
A one percent Treaty You have enough nuclear weapons to kill everyone thirteen times.

2
00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:10.340
Here's how to settle for twelve point eight seven times and use the savings on medicine.

3
00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:19.280
Controversial, apparently. Your one percent Treaty is a piece of paper that says every signatory nation redirects one percent

4
00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:26.800
of its annual military budget to enable any patient to participate in pragmatic clinical trials for the most promising new

5
00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:36.000
treatments. The Math. The Problem: The world spends two point seven two trillion dollars a year on military forces.

6
00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:44.950
The Hack: If every nation reduces military spending by one percent *at the same time*, the balance of power remains identical.

7
00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:54.640
No country becomes more vulnerable. The Prize: That one percent, twenty-seven point two billion dollars a year, is redirected

8
00:00:54.640 --> 00:01:02.300
to fund clinical trials for every human disease. For comparison, twenty-seven point two billion dollars is roughly what

9
00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:07.400
Americans spend on Valentine's Day, and what humans spend on pet food every three months.

10
00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:16.060
How It Works. Funding: Creates a twenty-seven point two billion dollar a year revenue stream for the one percent Treaty

11
00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:26.380
Fund. Execution: A network of Decentralized Institutes of Health subsidizes patient participation in a decentralized F.D.A.,

12
00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:31.440
clinical trials that are forty-four point one times cheaper than the current model.

13
00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:41.180
Governance: Global population via Wishocracy, with transparent auditable rules and automated fund allocation.

14
00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:46.920
Transparency: All research, data, and spending published on public ledger.

15
00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:54.340
This Isn't a Trade-Off. It's like if everyone in your neighborhood spent their life savings on attack dogs, and then called

16
00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:58.120
it a "safe community" because all the dogs are equally vicious.

17
00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:07.100
When every nation builds one percent fewer explosion devices *at the same time*, you get two good things: You become safer.

18
00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:11.740
The global "you might all die by accident" temperature drops one percent.

19
00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:16.800
Fewer chances of someone pressing the wrong button, which happens more than you'd think.

20
00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:25.920
You stop dying from stupid things. That twenty-seven point two billion dollars pays sick people to try treatments instead

21
00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:34.220
of waiting for death. Economists call this a Kaldor-Hicks improvement, which is their way of saying "the winners win more

22
00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:41.720
than the losers lose, and we've decided that counts." In practice, some defense contractors lose one percent of their contracts.

23
00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:47.600
In exchange, they and everyone they love become less likely to die of cancer.

24
00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:52.620
Most people consider this a good trade. Defense contractors are still doing the math.

25
00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:58.740
The Dead Capital Problem (Or: Why Bombs Are the Worst Investment Since Tulips).

26
00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:03.780
When countries "invest" in military hardware, they're not buying assets.

27
00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:08.120
They're buying very expensive paperweights that occasionally explode.

28
00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:14.980
A bridge. Generates economic activity daily (trucks move, people commute, value flows).

29
00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:20.220
Lasts fifty to one hundred years. Makes everyone richer. Pretty to look at.

30
00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:29.210
A missile. Sits in a hole doing nothing. Costs one point five million dollars a year just to keep it from rotting.

31
00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:34.120
Can only destroy things (including itself). Makes everyone nervous.

32
00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:42.460
This is what economists call "dead capital": resources trapped in a form that can't generate returns.

33
00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:50.120
It's like converting your house into a pile of T.N.T. Impressive, but you can't live in it, rent it out, or do anything

34
00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:56.600
except eventually blow it up, at which point you have neither a house nor T.N.T.

35
00:03:56.600 --> 00:04:04.440
As President Eisenhower (a five-star General who knew about killing professionally) put it: "Every gun that is made, every

36
00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:13.860
warship launched, every rocket fired signifies, in the final sense, a theft from those who hunger and are not fed, those

37
00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:22.200
who are cold and are not clothed." He literally ran the biggest military in history, and even HE thought you were being

38
00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:31.220
ridiculous about this. A one percent treaty doesn't ask you to spend twenty-seven point two billion dollars curing diseases.

39
00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:39.300
It asks you to redirect twenty-seven point two billion dollars from the least useful one percent of your military budget

40
00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:45.420
to things that make you richer and less dead. The Blueprint.

41
00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:49.900
Treaties are pieces of paper that countries sign when they want to pretend they'll behave.

42
00:04:50.980 --> 00:04:57.380
Sometimes they work (you haven't had World War Three yet). Sometimes they don't (you've had about two hundred other wars).

43
00:04:57.880 --> 00:05:03.720
But they're the only tool you have for getting countries to do anything together besides kill each other.

44
00:05:04.840 --> 00:05:13.660
What Each Country Pays. Every signatory nation contributes exactly one percent of their military budget to the one percent

45
00:05:13.660 --> 00:05:21.680
Treaty Fund. The U.S.A. contributes eight point eight-six billion dollars (won't even notice, they've failed to account

46
00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:29.620
for over two point four-six trillion dollars). China contributes two point nine-six billion dollars (what they spend on

47
00:05:29.620 --> 00:05:37.440
military parades). India contributes eight hundred and thirty-five million dollars (one aircraft carrier's wine budget).

48
00:05:38.260 --> 00:05:42.860
Russia contributes one point zero-nine billion dollars (Putin's shirt budget).

49
00:05:43.660 --> 00:05:48.600
Saudi Arabia contributes seven hundred and fifty-four million dollars (one prince's yacht).

50
00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:52.680
The U.K. contributes six hundred and eighty-six million dollars.

51
00:05:53.160 --> 00:05:58.530
France contributes five hundred and sixty-three million dollars (military cheese allocation).

52
00:05:59.140 --> 00:06:04.520
And so on... Why This Is Actually About National Security (No, Really).

53
00:06:05.340 --> 00:06:10.420
What actually threatens nations today: Real Threats (That Actually Kill People).

54
00:06:10.820 --> 00:06:18.360
One. Pandemics: COVID killed more Americans than World War Two, Korea, and Vietnam combined.

55
00:06:19.140 --> 00:06:26.940
Two. Cancer: Kills ten million globally per year. Three. Heart Disease: eighteen million annual deaths.

56
00:06:27.760 --> 00:06:33.500
Four. Dementia: Destroying the minds of every nation's elderly (and sometimes their leaders).

57
00:06:34.380 --> 00:06:38.820
Five. Climate Disasters: Making entire regions uninhabitable.

58
00:06:39.080 --> 00:06:44.700
Six. Mental Health Crisis: More people kill themselves than die in wars.

59
00:06:45.780 --> 00:06:51.460
Overfunded Threats (That Countries Spend Trillions On). Other Countries Attacking: It happens.

60
00:06:51.500 --> 00:06:58.660
It's real. Ask Ukraine. But disease kills two hundred and twenty-five times more people every year, and you're funding them

61
00:06:58.660 --> 00:07:03.940
in the opposite ratio. Terrorists: Kill fewer people than furniture accidents.

62
00:07:04.600 --> 00:07:11.160
But here's the part your defense budget doesn't advertise: military occupation is the primary *cause* of terrorism.

63
00:07:11.840 --> 00:07:18.360
Robert Pape at the University of Chicago analyzed every suicide terrorist attack from nineteen eighty to two thousand and

64
00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:25.800
three and found that ninety-five percent were responses to foreign military occupation, not religious ideology.

65
00:07:26.280 --> 00:07:34.040
Half were completely secular. Your eight trillion dollar War on Terror killed nine hundred thousand people and global terrorist

66
00:07:34.040 --> 00:07:39.680
attacks went from about one thousand per year to nearly seventeen thousand per year by twenty fourteen.

67
00:07:40.460 --> 00:07:45.760
Nearly all of them targeted democracies that had stationed military forces in the attackers' homeland.

68
00:07:46.660 --> 00:07:52.080
You spent eight trillion dollars making the problem seventeen times worse.

69
00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:57.620
Your military bases aren't protecting you from terrorism. They're the reason it's pointed at you.

70
00:07:58.260 --> 00:08:04.020
A one percent treaty addresses the real threats. It doesn't weaken your ability to handle the conventional ones.

71
00:08:04.520 --> 00:08:11.320
You keep ninety-nine percent of your weapons. If ninety-nine percent of your military can't handle the job, the last one

72
00:08:11.320 --> 00:08:16.160
percent wasn't going to save you. Besides, you've done far bigger cuts before.

73
00:08:16.600 --> 00:08:24.420
After World War Two, the U.S. slashed military spending by eighty-seven point six percent in two years (from one point four

74
00:08:24.420 --> 00:08:29.100
two trillion dollars to one hundred and seventy-six billion dollars in today's dollars).

75
00:08:29.900 --> 00:08:36.500
G.M. went from building Cadillacs to building B-twenty-four bombers, won the war, and went back to Cadillacs.

76
00:08:37.480 --> 00:08:40.820
Frigidaire stopped making machine guns and returned to refrigerators.

77
00:08:41.419 --> 00:08:47.780
Nobody held a congressional hearing about whether the refrigerator industry could survive without the machine gun contracts.

78
00:08:48.360 --> 00:08:51.820
Frigidaire just made refrigerators again. It wasn't complicated.

79
00:08:52.620 --> 00:08:56.500
It was only complicated later, when the machine gun industry hired lobbyists.

80
00:08:57.040 --> 00:09:00.880
The economy didn't collapse. It produced the greatest boom in your history.

81
00:09:01.500 --> 00:09:09.960
Today you're spending thirty point six times the pre-World War Two baseline in inflation-adjusted dollars: twenty-nine billion

82
00:09:09.960 --> 00:09:17.880
dollars in nineteen thirty-nine versus eight hundred and eighty-six billion dollars today, during peacetime, with no existential

83
00:09:17.880 --> 00:09:26.120
threat requiring it. Your defense industry learned how to make peacetime feel like wartime (see Regulatory Capture).

84
00:09:26.180 --> 00:09:33.760
You're asking to redirect one percent of that. The Rebranding Campaign: From "Weakness" to "Strategic Genius".

85
00:09:35.360 --> 00:09:41.520
Politicians hate looking weak. It's their biggest fear, right after having to use their own healthcare system.

86
00:09:41.940 --> 00:09:49.000
So you don't frame this as "reducing military spending." You frame it as: "The Strategic Health Defense Initiative".

87
00:09:49.000 --> 00:09:49.380
The old frame ("We're cutting defense by one percent") is political suicide.

88
00:09:49.380 --> 00:09:51.120
The new frame ("We're building a biodefense shield against the real threats") is political genius.

89
00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:56.380
Same policy. Different adjective. Your species can be manipulated with adjectives.

90
00:09:56.960 --> 00:10:01.560
This is simultaneously the most depressing and most useful fact about you.

91
00:10:02.420 --> 00:10:09.780
The Talking Points That Make Hawks Sound Like Doves. For Conservatives: "This is the ultimate America First policy.

92
00:10:10.820 --> 00:10:15.360
We're protecting American lives from the Chinese virus and any future biological threats.

93
00:10:16.280 --> 00:10:19.400
It's the Strategic Defense Initiative for the twenty-first century."

94
00:10:20.320 --> 00:10:25.180
For Liberals: "We're redirecting the tools of war to the cause of healing.

95
00:10:25.740 --> 00:10:33.700
This is how we build a more just and equitable world." For Nationalists: "Our nation grows stronger when our people stop

96
00:10:33.900 --> 00:10:38.720
dying of diseases we can prevent. A healthy population is a powerful population."

97
00:10:39.700 --> 00:10:47.820
For Economists: "The return on investment, or R.O.I., on the one billion dollar campaign to pass it is eighty-four point

98
00:10:47.820 --> 00:10:53.960
eight million to one. On paper, that beats every public health intervention on record."

99
00:10:54.920 --> 00:11:01.240
For Conspiracy Theorists: "Big Pharma hates this one weird trick that makes medicine basically free."

100
00:11:02.160 --> 00:11:06.680
For Everyone: "Your mom has cancer. This might cure it. Sign here."

101
00:11:07.960 --> 00:11:16.740
The Money Flow. The one percent Treaty Fund. Twenty-seven point two billion dollars annually from military budgets flows

102
00:11:16.740 --> 00:11:24.740
into the one percent Treaty Fund. An eighty, ten, ten automatic split divides it before any funds reach discretionary spending:

103
00:11:25.440 --> 00:11:32.080
Pragmatic Clinical Trials receive eighty percent of the funds, totaling twenty-one point eight billion dollars for patient

104
00:11:32.080 --> 00:11:41.040
subsidies, R and D, and pandemic prep. VICTORY Incentive Alignment Bond Returns receive ten percent, totaling two point

105
00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:48.820
seven two billion dollars for perpetual investor payments. Incentive Alignment Bond Political Incentives receive ten percent,

106
00:11:49.400 --> 00:11:53.920
totaling two point seven two billion dollars for rewards for supporting legislators.

107
00:11:55.100 --> 00:11:58.860
Notice what this does: it disentangles money from influence.

108
00:11:59.480 --> 00:12:07.240
In current government budgets, the people who provide the money (lobbyists, donors) also control where it goes.

109
00:12:07.340 --> 00:12:11.610
Money buys influence, influence redirects money, and the loop is invisible.

110
00:12:12.340 --> 00:12:18.220
Here, investors get money (ten percent). Politicians get career incentives (ten percent).

111
00:12:18.920 --> 00:12:22.260
Neither group touches the eighty percent that goes to clinical trials.

112
00:12:22.640 --> 00:12:27.650
The corruption is capped, transparent, and separated from the public goods funding.

113
00:12:28.380 --> 00:12:34.470
The eighty percent for pragmatic clinical trials is insulated from investor and political capture.

114
00:12:34.640 --> 00:12:41.800
Patients direct trial-participation spending by joining studies, while Wishocracy governs infrastructure and other shared

115
00:12:41.800 --> 00:12:48.100
medical public goods, not by a committee of twelve people in a conference room who haven't had a new idea since nineteen

116
00:12:48.100 --> 00:12:54.160
eighty-seven. Every human still gets direct say on the part that actually requires collective choice.

117
00:12:55.080 --> 00:13:04.580
Yes, even Florida Man. Year One Projected Outcomes. Only one point nine million participate in drug trials annually out

118
00:13:04.580 --> 00:13:12.420
of two point four billion people with chronic disease. With a one percent treaty plus a decentralized F.D.A.: The current

119
00:13:12.420 --> 00:13:19.660
cost per patient of forty-one thousand dollars drops to nine hundred and twenty-nine dollars with the treaty, making it

120
00:13:19.660 --> 00:13:26.540
forty-four point one times cheaper. Government trial funding of four point five billion dollars would see an additional

121
00:13:26.540 --> 00:13:33.520
twenty-seven point two billion dollars from the treaty. The number of annual participants would increase from one point

122
00:13:33.520 --> 00:13:39.870
nine million patients to twenty-three point four million, a twelve point three times increase.

123
00:13:40.280 --> 00:13:47.100
The Incentive Structure. The military-industrial complex gets to keep ninety-nine percent of their budget (still plenty

124
00:13:47.100 --> 00:13:54.340
for bombs), pivot one percent to biotech (the health market is growing; the bomb market just got one percent smaller), look

125
00:13:54.340 --> 00:14:01.420
like heroes (rare for arms dealers), invest in the fund (VICTORY Incentive Alignment Bonds pay two hundred and seventy-two

126
00:14:01.420 --> 00:14:06.280
percent annually), and not die of cancer (even arms dealers get cancer).

127
00:14:06.820 --> 00:14:13.040
Especially the not dying of cancer part. The C.E.O. of Lockheed Martin has the same cells you do.

128
00:14:13.360 --> 00:14:20.520
Those cells malfunction on the same schedule. No amount of missile production has ever cured a tumor.

129
00:14:20.620 --> 00:14:27.100
Lockheed Martin Presents: The F thirty-five Cancer Killer. Boeing Proud Sponsor of: Not Dying of Alzheimer's.

130
00:14:27.200 --> 00:14:31.750
Raytheon Technologies: Now With one percent Less Death. They keep their contracts.

131
00:14:31.880 --> 00:14:36.940
They keep their profits. They just point one percent of their death machines at actual death.

132
00:14:37.700 --> 00:14:43.920
What Happens If This Actually Works. The Endgame. A one percent treaty isn't the goal.

133
00:14:43.940 --> 00:14:50.680
It's the proof of concept. I've watched roughly eight hundred and forty-seven civilizations reach this exact fork in the

134
00:14:50.680 --> 00:14:57.400
road; the ones still around all started with something like one percent, and the ones that didn't are geological features

135
00:14:57.400 --> 00:15:04.500
now. Once your species gets a taste of "spending money on things that help instead of things that explode," you'll want

136
00:15:04.500 --> 00:15:12.120
more. The Ratchet Effect. Once a one percent treaty is signed and the first cures start rolling out, the system becomes

137
00:15:12.120 --> 00:15:16.320
more valuable. Every five to ten years, you hold another global vote.

138
00:15:16.800 --> 00:15:19.920
"Grandma's dementia is manageable now. Want to try another half-percent?".

139
00:15:21.020 --> 00:15:27.240
More cures lead to more popular support, which leads to more votes for more funding, which leads to more cures.

140
00:15:27.580 --> 00:15:32.180
The endgame: countries bragging about their cancer survival rates instead of their missile counts.

141
00:15:33.100 --> 00:15:39.120
For quantitative projections of health and economic impacts, see impact dot war on disease dot org.

142
00:15:39.920 --> 00:15:43.860
Avoiding Treaty Pitfalls. Treaties fail in predictable ways.

143
00:15:44.380 --> 00:15:51.780
They all fail for the same boring reasons. Here's how this one avoids the common killers: Reservation Games.

144
00:15:52.260 --> 00:15:55.720
Countries love to sign treaties then add exceptions that gut them.

145
00:15:56.040 --> 00:15:59.180
It's like joining a gym but reserving the right to never go.

146
00:15:59.620 --> 00:16:04.020
Solution: No reservations permitted. All or nothing. Take it or leave it.

147
00:16:04.800 --> 00:16:10.380
Ratification Delays. Sign with fanfare, take a photo, then let it die in committee forever.

148
00:16:11.040 --> 00:16:15.500
Your species calls this "diplomacy.". Solution: Provisional application.

149
00:16:16.100 --> 00:16:20.460
Takes effect on signature. Ratification just formalizes what already started.

150
00:16:21.280 --> 00:16:28.760
Creative Accounting. Reclassify "military" spending as "peacekeeping" or "homeland security" to dodge obligations.

151
00:16:29.600 --> 00:16:35.360
Solution: Incentive Alignment Bonds, or I.A.B.s, make creative accounting self-defeating.

152
00:16:35.680 --> 00:16:42.540
Politicians who benefit from treaty compliance have personal financial incentive to ensure their nation's full one percent

153
00:16:42.540 --> 00:16:49.420
contribution flows through. Reclassifying military spending to dodge obligations reduces I.A.B.

154
00:16:49.680 --> 00:16:54.920
returns for that nation's own legislators, the same people who control budget classifications.

155
00:16:56.180 --> 00:17:01.840
Parliamentary Obstacles. Executive signs, legislature refuses to implement.

156
00:17:02.680 --> 00:17:11.130
Solution: The treaty obligates the percentage; Congress implements via the annual National Defense Authorization Act (N.D.A.A.).

157
00:17:12.200 --> 00:17:18.890
After Medellin v. Texas (two thousand and eight), the Supreme Court made clear that not all treaties are self-executing,

158
00:17:19.579 --> 00:17:27.260
so the treaty doesn't try to bypass Congress. It creates a binding international obligation that Congress fulfills through

159
00:17:27.260 --> 00:17:31.900
its normal appropriations process, the same way NATO's spending commitments work.

160
00:17:32.880 --> 00:17:40.120
The I.A.B. mechanism ensures legislators have a personal incentive to include the one percent line item every year.

161
00:17:41.380 --> 00:17:50.140
Summary. A one percent treaty is a piece of paper that says "Let's spend one percent less on killing and one percent more

162
00:17:50.140 --> 00:17:55.240
on not dying.". That's it. That's the whole thing. It doesn't end war.

163
00:17:55.500 --> 00:17:59.300
It doesn't destroy the military. It doesn't create world peace.

164
00:17:59.540 --> 00:18:04.860
It just moves the FIRST one percent of your murder budget to your survival budget.

165
00:18:05.660 --> 00:18:12.960
If you can't agree on that, if you can't agree that spending one penny less on death and one penny more on life is a good

166
00:18:12.960 --> 00:18:20.600
idea, then you deserve whatever's coming. Addendum: The Actual Treaty Text (First Draft).

167
00:18:21.700 --> 00:18:29.240
THE TREATY FOR THE ONE PERCENT REALLOCATION OF MILITARY EXPENDITURES TOWARDS BIOMEDICAL RESEARCH AND DEVELOPMENT FOR THE

168
00:18:29.240 --> 00:18:38.600
PREVENTION OF HUMAN SUFFERING AND DEATH (Or: The "Let's Not Die" Treaty, for short) WHEREAS, humanity spends two point seven

169
00:18:38.600 --> 00:18:48.000
two trillion dollars annually on methods of killing itself; WHEREAS, this seems somewhat counterproductive; WHEREAS, diseases

170
00:18:48.000 --> 00:18:56.140
kill more people than all wars combined and don't even have the decency to be quick about it; WHEREAS, we have nuclear weapons

171
00:18:56.140 --> 00:19:06.580
sufficient to end civilization thirteen times but haven't cured Alzheimer's once; WHEREAS, this is embarrassing; NOW, THEREFORE,

172
00:19:06.680 --> 00:19:14.660
the undersigned nations agree to try a different approach, as follows: Article One: Each signatory shall redirect exactly

173
00:19:14.660 --> 00:19:22.840
one percent of its annual military budget to the one percent Treaty Fund for allocation to pragmatic clinical trials.

174
00:19:23.640 --> 00:19:28.860
Article Two: Transfers shall be automatic, immediate, and irrevocable.

175
00:19:28.900 --> 00:19:35.860
No "we'll get to it later.". Article Three: Percentages may increase but never decrease.

176
00:19:35.880 --> 00:19:44.240
This is a ratchet, not a yo-yo. Article Four: Compliance shall be verified by public ledger and independent audits.

177
00:19:44.300 --> 00:19:50.900
No creative accounting. Article Five: Non-compliant parties lose access to I.A.B.

178
00:19:50.940 --> 00:19:54.940
benefits and treaty fund advantages. Your opponents get what you forfeit.

179
00:19:56.020 --> 00:20:00.140
Article Six: Success metrics trigger mandatory percentage reviews.

180
00:20:00.940 --> 00:20:07.780
When it works, we do more. Article Seven: Citizens have standing to enforce via domestic courts.

181
00:20:07.860 --> 00:20:15.440
You can sue your own government for non-compliance. Article Eight: Withdrawal requires unanimous consent of all parties

182
00:20:15.440 --> 00:20:24.420
plus ten-year notice. Good luck with that. Article Nine: This treaty supersedes all conflicting domestic law.

183
00:20:24.820 --> 00:20:31.620
Yes, even that law. Article Ten: Entry into force upon signature by two states.

184
00:20:32.240 --> 00:20:39.920
You only need two countries brave enough to go first. IN WITNESS WHEREOF, the undersigned, being of sound mind and tired

185
00:20:39.920 --> 00:20:45.600
of watching their loved ones die of preventable diseases, have executed this Treaty.

186
00:20:45.600 --> 00:20:50.800
Signed this day, in the year of our ongoing confusion. Nation Name.

187
00:20:50.800 --> 00:20:56.380
"We choose life, I guess". P.S. Yes, this includes space weapons.

188
00:20:57.060 --> 00:20:57.460
Nice try.
